---
import ThemeSvg from './ThemeSvg.astro';
import light from '../../diagrams/resume/flow.svg?url';
import dark from '../../diagrams/resume/flow-dark.svg?url';
import path from 'node:path';

const dir = path.resolve('src/diagrams/resume');

const alt =
	'Software stores it. People read it. Step 1, you apply: apply early, one manager reads about the first 150. Step 2, the ATS stores it: at many companies, it stores your resume rather than auto-rejecting it. Step 3, a recruiter skims it: companies, titles, and dates get read first. Step 4, a manager reads it, fast, often against hundreds of other resumes. A dashed branch shows where automatic cuts happen, at knockout questions. Form answers can cut you automatically.';
---

<figure class="eep-fig wide eep-theme-fig" id="fig-1">
	<ThemeSvg
		light={light}
		dark={dark}
		lightFile={path.join(dir, 'flow.svg')}
		darkFile={path.join(dir, 'flow-dark.svg')}
		alt={alt}
	/>
	<figcaption>
		<span class="eep-fignum">Fig 1</span>
		<span>
			At many companies, automatic cuts come from knockout questions on the
			form, not your resume text. Exact steps vary by company. Sources:
			r/EngineeringResumes hiring managers, recruiters on X.
		</span>
	</figcaption>
</figure>
